Metrics
Let’s quickly look at some migration metrics.
Across the Auth Fleet, we currently have approximately 820 web-based automated test cases.
Compared to previous migration efforts that took over a year to complete, this migration has progressed significantly faster.
Within just three to four weeks, we have already achieved approximately 90% migration at the fleet level.
This demonstrates the acceleration that AI-assisted migration can bring to large automation programs.
Another important point is that all existing assets are preserved during migration. This includes feature files, locator definitions, test data files, configuration files, and application parameters.
The goal moving forward is not just migration but also increasing automation coverage further through AI-driven approaches.


Future
Looking ahead, the framework currently supports web automation and is ready for migration activities.
API automation and mobile automation are currently under POC and will be integrated into future releases.
For new test cases, users can simply provide Jira information in the .environment file and specify the target platform. The framework can then automatically generate the required artifacts, including feature files, locators, specs, and test data.
Traditionally, onboarding a new feature into automation required significant manual effort. With Playwright MCP and AI, this process becomes faster.
Our next goal is to integrate the entire SDLC workflow. This includes starting from Jira intake, test creation, automation generation, execution, validation, and finally code check-in, all with minimal manual intervention.


Conclusion
To conclude, this migration is not just a framework upgrade from Selenium to Playwright. It is a step towards AI-assisted test automation and productivity.
By combining Playwright’s stability with MCP, we are reducing manual effort and creating a foundation for future AI-driven automation.
Thank you everyone for your time.
